# outline_group_member

Manages one user's membership and group permission in a manually maintained Outline group. It does not create or delete the group or user, change the user's workspace role, or manage collection access. Destroy removes only this group/user pair, not other memberships.

Use an unrestricted API key owned by an active workspace admin. The provider verifies the caller for every operation, including refresh and import. The resource targets Outline 1.10.1 only.

Groups linked through `externalId` or synchronized from an external identity provider cannot be managed or imported with this resource. The provider checks the group before reads and mutations and refuses externally linked or synchronized groups.

## Example usage

{{ tffile "examples/resources/outline_group_member/resource.tf" }}

Replace the UUIDs with the existing group's and user's IDs. You can also reference `outline_group.engineering.id` and `outline_user.alice.id` when Terraform manages those parents. Pending invited users can join groups before their first sign-in. No password, SMTP service, or sign-in is required; the [user resource](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/glitchedmob/outline/latest/docs/resources/user) can provision them with `suppress_email = true`.

`group_id` and `user_id` are required canonical lowercase, nonzero UUIDs. Changing either requires replacement. The computed Terraform `id` is `group_id/user_id`, with exactly one slash. It is not Outline's returned `GroupUser.id`, which uses `userId-groupId`.

`permission` is optional and computed, with a default of `member`. Valid values are `member` and `admin`. Omitting it or removing it from configuration reconciles the membership to `member` on the next apply. Omission does not leave the permission unmanaged, including after importing an admin membership. Configure `permission = "admin"` to retain that permission.

The API-key owner's own membership is supported. Outline 1.10.1 treats an active workspace admin as a group admin regardless of their stored group permission. Changing or removing this membership does not change their workspace role. The pinned release has no last-group-admin or group-owner protection for these operations; the provider does not impose one. Review permission changes and removals before applying.

## Creation and updates

Create checks the parents and every page of the group's memberships before writing. If the pair already exists, creation fails and requires import. It then calls `groups.add_user` with the intended permission explicitly. Permission changes use `groups.update_user`, also with an explicit permission. Destroy uses `groups.remove_user` for only the configured group/user pair.

Outline 1.10.1 implements `groups.add_user` as an upsert. Another writer can add the same pair after the preflight and before the write, and the provider's call can overwrite that writer's permission. The API has no atomic create-only operation to prevent this race. Coordinate writers for each pair, including other Terraform states and manual changes. Do not manage the same pair with multiple resources.

Reads use the complete paginated `groups.memberships` response, never the group list's membership preview. The provider validates every page, consistent totals, duplicate memberships, and matching parent IDs before using a result or declaring the pair absent. A malformed or incomplete response is an error even if the target pair appeared on an earlier page.

Refresh removes the resource from state only when a complete validated membership list proves the pair absent, or the existing release-verified group/user read helpers prove a parent absent. A `403` or `404` from a membership endpoint never proves absence. Authorization failures, failed parent checks, and malformed pages retain state and return an error.

## Failed-create recovery

After a successful preflight, the provider retains the trusted `group_id/user_id` in state before attempting the create write. A timeout, error response, or failed validation can occur after the server commits the membership. The retained ID is a recovery handle, not proof that creation succeeded. Failed responses preserve state, and Terraform taints failed creates.

Inspect the actual membership and fix the reported error before retrying. If the pair exists and state still contains it, untaint the resource to reconcile it in place, then review the plan:

```shell
terraform untaint outline_group_member.alice
terraform plan
```

Use your resource's address. Do not accept replacement blindly, since it removes the pair before adding it again. If state was lost, import the existing pair instead. If the pair is absent, a successful refresh removes the retained state so Terraform can create it again.

